# Working on <%= name %>

This is a Kex package. Kex looks like Ruby but is a statically typed,
immutable, functional language: Ruby and Elixir habits produce code that does
not compile.

**Before writing or editing any Kex, run `kex --skill`** and read it. It is the
language guide the compiler carries, so it describes exactly the Kex installed
here. `kex --skill <topic>` goes deeper: `functions`, `make`, `control`,
`errors`, `modules`, `processes`, `testing`, `style`, `tey`.

## While working

- Look names up, never guess them: `kex -s List`, `kex -s List.find`,
  `kex -s find`.
- Check after every edit: `kex -C --source-root src <file>`.
- Run the specs: `tey test`. One case: `tey test --only "<describe> > <it>"`.
<% if library %>
- This is a library: its modules live under `src/` and it has no entrypoint.
<% end %>
<% if !library %>
- Run the program: `tey run`.
<% end %>
- `tey test --interpret` needs no Erlang.

## Layout

- `package.kex` is the manifest: name, version, dependencies, commands.
- `src/` holds the modules. `module Shop.Cart` is the file `src/shop/cart.kex`.
- `spec/` holds the specs, directly in it, named `<name>.spec.kex`.
- `tey.lock` is committed. `ebin/` is build output.
